0. Epistemic status and scope

This paper is a purely conceptual and mathematical work. It reports:

· No physical experiments. No human subjects were recruited; no behavioral responses were observed under controlled conditions; no ratings were collected.
· No model training. No models were fine-tuned, RLHF'd, constitutional-AI'd, or otherwise modified. All references to "training," "preference data," and "constitutions" refer to published work by others, cited as literature.
· No code. No implementation, no inference run, no prompt-set execution.

Consequently:

· Every formal result below is either a definition, an axiom, a theorem proven from stated definitions and axioms, or an explicitly marked empirical hypothesis. The paper does not claim that every numbered item is a theorem.
· Where the paper states a hypothesis, it is marked H or E and labeled untested or empirical. Where it states an axiom, it is marked A and labeled normative. Where it states a theorem, it is marked P and proven.
· The empirical program specified in §7 is specified, not executed. Any future execution would require the apparatus described there, which does not exist in this paper.

Changes from v0.4. This version responds to a third round of critical review in eight respects:

1. P1/E1 is downgraded. E1 is restated as a compatibility claim, not an empirically supported premise. The value-induction literature is compatible with, but does not establish, non-injectivity of the state-to-claim map.
2. P2 handles the λ = 0 edge case. The equality condition now explicitly includes λ = 0.
