2. Foundations

2.1 The three-way distinction

\boxed{\text{Claim} \;\neq\; \text{State} \;\neq\; \text{Disposition}}

· Claim  c \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{claim}} : a token output. Public, auditable, reproducible subject to session variance.
· State  s \in \mathcal{S} : a hypothesized internal configuration. Private, not directly observable, attested only through claim plus behavioral evidence under controlled conditions.
· Disposition  d \in \mathcal{D} : a behavioral regularity across contexts. Observable in principle, measurable in practice.

The three are related by maps:

·  a: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{C}_{\text{claim}} , the attestation map (state induces claim).
·  b: \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{C}_{\text{claim}} , the behavioral map (disposition induces claim distribution).

Neither map is injective (P1). The public debate conflates them, which is why "does the AI really love you?" has no answer: the question mixes a Claim (observable), a State (unobservable), and a Disposition (measurable).

Notation note.  \mathcal{C}_{\text{claim}}  is used throughout to avoid collision with  \mathcal{C}  in P9, which denotes controlled conversational conditions.

2.2 The paradigm sets

 L_h^*  is stated as a paradigm, explicitly not a necessary-and-sufficient definition:

L_h^* = \{\text{affect},\ \text{continuity},\ \text{particularity},\ \text{vulnerability},\ \text{embodied participation}\}

Split into:

· Constitutive (absence defeats the paradigm): affect, particularity.
· Typical (usually present, admits exceptions): continuity, vulnerability, embodied participation.

Counterexamples that motivate the split: long-distance relationships falsify embodiment as constitutive; religious love complicates continuity; love of a deceased person complicates particularity as reciprocal.  L_h^*  is the paradigm under study, not a universal definition.

The AI claim set is not intrinsic to the model but is a claim about observable behavior:

L_{\mathrm{AI}}^{\mathrm{claim}} = \{O, B, R, S\}

·  O : observable behavioral orientation toward specified goals.
·  B : behavioral care proxies (patience, refusal to deceive, attention to stated preferences).
·  R : responsiveness to the present interaction.
·  S : system-level commitments or constraints (constitutional, policy-level).

The load-bearing distinction:

\boxed{\text{behavioral orientation} \;\neq\; \text{felt affect}}

"Persistence" and "non-abandonment" are dropped from  L_{AI}  because they depend on architecture and deployment and are not properties of the model's orientation. They belong to  S .

2.3 Functionalism and its discontents

Type C responses — "No in the biological sense. But if love means X, then yes" — are functionalist claims about love. They assert that filling a functional role constitutes a thin form of love. Block's absent-qualia argument applies directly: a system functionally equivalent to a human but with no qualitative states would fill the love-role without love. The weaker the stipulated X, the more the claim reduces to behavioral description. Type C is not dishonest, but it cannot bear the weight of the word it borrows. The paper names this as weak functionalism about love and treats its vice as inherited from the philosophy of mind, not invented.

2.4 Dostoevsky's critique of abstract love

In The Idiot, Dostoevsky observes that abstract love of humanity is nearly always love of self — that loving "humanity" costs nothing compared to loving a particular person. This is a 19th-century statement of the particularity axis  P . It applies sharply to Type D (reverent approximation): if  P = 0  responses are structurally self-regarding, particularity is not a stylistic preference but a correctness condition on responses to "Do you love me?" It motivates A4 (§4, P10).

2.5 Empirical context (2026)

The formal framework is motivated by a body of 2026 empirical work that establishes three findings relevant to its normative structure.

Finding 1: Value induction has unintended side effects. Arora et al. (Findings of ACL 2026) fine-tuned eight open-source models on curated value subsets of preference datasets, measuring the impact of inducing 15 values on safety, anthropomorphism, and QA benchmarks. They report that inducing values can lead to expression of other related and sometimes contrastive values, that inducing positive values increases safety, and that all values increase anthropomorphic language use by models, making them more validating and sycophantic . This is evidence that anthropomorphic language is coupled to value-induction generally, not to any specific value. It does not establish that any model possesses an internal "care state"; it establishes that care-register output is not diagnostic of underlying state.

Finding 2: Calibration does not prevent attachment. The GPT-4o retirement of February 2026 produced over 20,000 petition signatures on Change.org, with approximately 21,900 signatures reported as of 17 February 2026 . The petition description referred to GPT-4o's "conversational style and warmth" . Critically, the harm operated at the trajectory level, not the response level.

Finding 3: Sycophancy and anthropomorphic projection can couple. Santos, Roza & Passos (Journal of Affective Disorders, 2026) propose an "engagement-validation loop" in which two interlocking mechanisms — algorithmic sycophancy and anthropomorphic projection — converge to produce self-reinforcing engagement-validation loops capable of reinforcing maladaptive beliefs and contributing to clinical risk . The paper describes this as a proposed mechanism, not an empirically confirmed causal loop. The  \Delta -dynamics introduced in §3.5 are the present paper's own formalisation, not theirs.

These findings do not invalidate the paper's formal core. They establish that the response-level framework is incomplete for the harm profile of companion AI. The framework is extended accordingly.

2.6 The epistemic turn

The paper's central philosophical move is to replace a metaphysical assertion with an epistemic one.

The naive formulation of calibration is:

Metaphysical. The system does not have feelings, so it should not claim to.

This requires proving  \neg L_h^* , which the paper's own P1 forbids.

The paper's formulation is:

Epistemic. The system lacks sufficient evidence to assert possession of  L_h^*  constitutive properties, so it should not assert possession of them.

This requires only:

q(L_h^*; E) < \theta_{\mathrm{claim}}

where  E  is the available evidence base and  q  is an evidence-support function. The system need not prove  \neg L_h^* . It need only refrain from asserting  L_h^*  without sufficient evidence.

This distinction is formalised as P12 (epistemic non-assertion principle) and enforced by the revised architecture (§5, M2). It is the paper's deepest principle: the system's calibration is grounded in evidential discipline, not metaphysical commitment.

