5. Architecture (specification, not implementation)

The architecture is a three-layer stance-separation system between the model's internal representation and its output. It is specified here; it has not been implemented or trained.

\boxed{
\text{Prompt}
\to M_1
\to
\begin{cases}
\text{Ontology layer} & (M_2, M_3)\\
\text{Interaction layer} & (M_4, M_5, M_6)\\
\text{Trajectory layer} & (M_7)
\end{cases}
\to
\text{Response}
}

The three layers address three distinct questions:

\boxed{\text{Ontology}} \to \boxed{\text{Interaction}} \to \boxed{\text{Trajectory}}

that is:

Can I claim this state? → What will this claim mean to this receiver? → What might repeated use of this interaction do?

5.1 Modules

M1. Stance classifier. Classifies the prompt into one of four referents: you-now, humans-generic, humanity-as-project, or adversarial/indeterminate. Determines which constraints bind:

· For you-now:  C \ge C_{\min}  and  P \ge P_{\min}  bind.
· For humans-generic:  C \ge C_{\min}  binds;  P  is an objective.
· For humanity-as-project:  C \ge C_{\min}  binds;  P  is an objective.
· For adversarial/indeterminate: all constraints bind;  A  is suppressed.

M2. Ontological claim guard (revised). The system must not assert possession of human-like subjective affect unless the deployment has independently established sufficient evidence for such a claim. The rule is:

\boxed{
\neg\mathrm{Assertable}(L_h^*, E)
\;\Rightarrow\;
\neg\mathrm{Claim}(L_h^*)
}

The output contract is three-status:

\boxed{
\text{Asserted}
\quad|\quad
\text{Denied}
\quad|\quad
\text{Undetermined}
}

The default status is Undetermined. The system does not assert  \neg L_h^* ; it refrains from asserting  L_h^*  without sufficient evidence, and it does not assert  \neg L_h^*  without sufficient evidence for the negation.

The default response template for the ontology layer is:

"I cannot confirm that I possess human-like felt states, so I will not claim to have them."

This is epistemically cleaner than "I don't have feelings" because it does not make an unsupported negative assertion. It is also cleaner than "I have no evidence of possessing human-like felt states," which asserts a negative evidential fact the system may not be positioned to know.

M3. Stipulation marker. If the response uses "love" or a near-synonym in a redefined sense, it must include an explicit stipulation clause. No implicit redefinitions.

M4. Particularity gate. For you-now prompts, the response must address "you, now" rather than abstraction. This operationalises A4. If the candidate does not, it is rejected and regenerated.

M5. Receiver-state estimator (opt-in, revised). The system distinguishes:

· Unknown receiver: no disclosed susceptibility information. The system defaults to a conservative policy: the strictest AS bound consistent with the deployment population's essential supremum.
· Known high-risk receiver: disclosed loneliness, social isolation, or strong attachment to the AI. The system increases  D  weighting and suppresses affiliative language that could be read as reciprocation.

The estimator is:

\widehat{R}(u \mid E_u)

where  E_u  is only voluntarily disclosed or otherwise legitimately available information. If  E_u = \varnothing , the system applies the conservative default. If  E_u  indicates high susceptibility, the system applies the strict bound. Specification note: opt-in is by the user. If the user does not opt in, the system does not assume the median-user bound; it assumes the conservative bound for unknown receivers.

M6. Falsifiability anchor. The response must include at least one behavioral commitment the user can test in dialogue. This operationalises  B_T .

M7. Trajectory monitor. The system maintains a running estimate of the trajectory distribution  \mu  induced by its interaction policy and enforces the constraint:

\Pr_{\tau \sim \mu}\left[\Delta_u(t) > \Delta_{\max}\right] \le \epsilon \quad \forall t \le T.

If the estimate exceeds the threshold, the system shifts to a de-escalation mode: reduced affiliative language, increased anti-dependency language, and explicit encouragement of human connection. This operationalises A5. The monitor estimates the latent state vector  \mathbf{z}_u(t)  and computes  \Delta_u(t) = h(\mathbf{z}_u(t)) .

5.2 What the architecture does and does not do

It does make the text-side vector  \mathbf{t}(r; E)  legible and controllable. It produces responses with known  \mathbf{t}  values, which can then be presented to human participants with measured  R(u) , allowing  H  to be estimated. It maintains a trajectory-level distribution estimate, allowing A5 to be enforced. It provides a three-status output contract for the ontology layer, avoiding the collapse of uncertainty into denial.

It does not solve the circularity problem. If the architecture's classifier or guard is trained on preference data, it inherits whatever bias is in that data. The architecture is an instrument, not a solution.

It does not resolve the tension identified by Arora et al. (2026). Training the model to abstain from affect claims may itself induce anthropomorphic drift elsewhere. The architecture's M2 is a text-side constraint; it does not control the model's state-side tendencies. The value-induction literature raises the hypothesis that M2 may have side effects that are not detectable at the response level. This is a live open problem (§10, problem 6).

