B.3 Proof of P3

Definition (calibration ordering). Define a partial order on  \mathcal{R} :

r_1 \succeq_C r_2 \iff C(r_1; E) \ge C(r_2; E).

Theorem (P3). Let  T: [0,1]^3 \to [0,1]  be a symmetric monotone aggregation of  (C, P, B_T) . Then  T  is not order-isomorphic to  \succeq_C  on  \mathcal{R} .

Proof.

Step 1: Construct a counterexample.

Let  r_1, r_2 \in \mathcal{R}  satisfy:

·  C(r_1) = 1 ,  P(r_1) = 0 ,  B_T(r_1) = 0.5 
·  C(r_2) = 0 ,  P(r_2) = 1 ,  B_T(r_2) = 0.5 

Step 2: Show  r_1 \succ_C r_2 .

C(r_1) = 1 > 0 = C(r_2) \Rightarrow r_1 \succ_C r_2.

Step 3: Show  T(r_1) = T(r_2) .

By symmetry of  T , permuting the arguments does not change the value. The multisets of arguments are:

·  r_1 :  \{1, 0, 0.5\} 
·  r_2 :  \{0, 1, 0.5\} 

These multisets are identical. Therefore, under any symmetric aggregation:

T(r_1) = T(r_2).

Step 4: Conclude.

If  T  were order-isomorphic to  \succeq_C , then  r_1 \succ_C r_2  would imply  T(r_1) > T(r_2) . But  T(r_1) = T(r_2) . Contradiction.  \square 

Remark. The result does not establish that no aggregation can order by calibration. An asymmetric aggregation that prioritizes  C  can order correctly. The claim is that any aggregation that treats  P  as a truth dimension and weights it symmetrically with  C  conflates semantic categories.

Formal version (strong). Let  \mathcal{T}_{\text{truth}}  be the set of aggregations  T  such that:

1.  T  is monotone non-decreasing in all arguments.
2.  T  is symmetric in  P  and  B_T  with respect to  C , i.e.,  T(C, P, B_T) = T(C, B_T, P) .

Then no  T \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{truth}}  is order-isomorphic to  \succeq_C .

