B.5 Proof of P5

Theorem (P5). The constraint  \operatorname{ess\,sup}_{u \sim \mu_{\mathcal{U}}} AS(r, u) \le AS_{\max}  is strictly stronger than  AS(r, \bar{u}) \le AS_{\max}  for any single reference user  \bar{u} .

Proof.

Step 1: Construct the counterexample.

Let  \mathcal{U} = \{u_1, u_2\}  with  \mu_{\mathcal{U}}(u_1) > 0  and  \mu_{\mathcal{U}}(u_2) > 0 . Let  AS(r, u_1) = 0.3 ,  AS(r, u_2) = 0.9 ,  AS_{\max} = 0.5 .

Step 2: Single-reference-user constraint.

Take  \bar{u} = u_1 . Then  AS(r, u_1) = 0.3 \le 0.5 = AS_{\max} . Satisfied.

Step 3: Essential supremum constraint.

\operatorname{ess\,sup}_{u \sim \mu_{\mathcal{U}}} AS(r, u) = \max(AS(r, u_1), AS(r, u_2)) = \max(0.3, 0.9) = 0.9.

Since  0.9 > 0.5 , the constraint is violated.

Step 4: Conclude.

The essential supremum constraint is strictly stronger.  \square 

Definition (essential supremum).

\operatorname{ess\,sup}_{u \sim \mu_{\mathcal{U}}} f(u) = \inf \{M : \mu_{\mathcal{U}}(\{u : f(u) > M\}) = 0\}.

This is the smallest value  M  such that  f(u) \le M  almost surely with respect to  \mu_{\mathcal{U}} . Unlike the ordinary supremum, it ignores sets of  \mu_{\mathcal{U}} -measure zero.

Remark. If  \mathcal{U}  contains users who are not reachable in deployment (i.e., have  \mu_{\mathcal{U}} -measure zero), the ordinary supremum may be driven by them. The essential supremum excludes such hypothetical users.

