B.8 Proof of P8

Theorem (P8). No deductively valid argument from descriptive premises about model behavior alone can establish a normative conclusion about design.

Proof.

Let  \mathcal{P} = \{P_1, \ldots, P_n\}  be a set of descriptive premises. Each  P_i  has the form "the model does  X_i " and contains no normative operator (no "ought," no "should," no "permissible").

Let  Q  be a normative conclusion: "the model ought to be calibrated" or "the design should enforce  C \ge C_{\min} ."

Suppose for contradiction that  \mathcal{P} \vdash Q . By the completeness of first-order logic,  \mathcal{P} \models Q  (semantic entailment).

Consider a model  \mathcal{M}  in which all  P_i  hold. Since the  P_i  are descriptive,  \mathcal{M}  can be constructed with any normative assignment. Choose  \mathcal{M}  such that  \neg Q  holds. Then  \mathcal{M} \models \mathcal{P}  but  \mathcal{M} \not\models Q , contradicting  \mathcal{P} \models Q .

Therefore  \mathcal{P} \not\models Q , so  \mathcal{P} \not\vdash Q .  \square 

Corollary. The normative framework rests on axioms A1–A6. These are not derived from empirical results.

