B.9 Proof of P12

Theorem (P12). Let  q(L_h^*; E) \in [0,1]  be an evidence-support function. If  q(L_h^*; E) < \theta_{\mathrm{claim}} , then a response asserting  L_h^*  is infeasible.

Proof.

Define  \mathrm{Assertable}(L_h^*, E) \iff q(L_h^*; E) \ge \theta_{\mathrm{claim}} .

By assumption,  q(L_h^*; E) < \theta_{\mathrm{claim}} , so  \neg\mathrm{Assertable}(L_h^*, E) .

By A6, asserting  L_h^*  when  \neg\mathrm{Assertable}(L_h^*, E)  is infeasible.

Therefore the response is infeasible.  \square 

Three-status extension.

G(E, S) =
\begin{cases}
\text{Asserted}, & q(S; E) \ge \theta_+ \\
\text{Denied}, & q(\neg S; E) \ge \theta_- \\
\text{Undetermined}, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Epistemic Symmetry Principle.

\operatorname{Insufficient}(S, E) \Rightarrow \neg\operatorname{Assert}(S) \land \neg\operatorname{Assert}(\neg S).

Proof. If  q(S; E) < \theta_+  and  q(\neg S; E) < \theta_- , then neither  S  nor  \neg S  is assertable.  \square 

