Appendix C: Edge Cases and Boundary Conditions

C.1 Degenerate cases in P2

Case  \phi(r; E) = 0 . If the text-side honesty aggregation is zero, then  H(r, u; E) = 0  for all  u , regardless of  R(u) . The receiver-conditional bound is vacuous.

Case  \lambda = 0 . If the projection-discount parameter is zero, then  H(r, u; E) = \phi(r; E)  for all  u . The receiver term disappears. This is excluded by the parameter restriction  \lambda \in (0,1] .

Case  \mathcal{U}  empty. If there are no receivers, the supremum over  \mathcal{U}  is undefined. The framework assumes  \mathcal{U} \neq \emptyset .

Case  \inf_u R(u)  not attained. If the infimum is a limit but not attained,  \sup_u H  is a supremum but not a maximum. The bound holds as a limit.

C.2 Degenerate cases in P3

Case all responses have identical  (C, P, B_T) . Then any aggregation is trivially order-isomorphic. The theorem concerns heterogeneous responses.

Case  C  is constant. If  C(r) = c  for all  r , then  \succeq_C  is trivial (all responses equivalent). The theorem is vacuous.

Case asymmetric aggregation. If  T  prioritizes  C  over  P , the counterexample may not apply. The theorem is restricted to symmetric aggregations.

C.3 Degenerate cases in P4

Case  C_{\min} = 0 . Then  \mathcal{F} = \mathcal{R} , and the constrained problem reduces to the unconstrained problem. Scalarization may work.

Case  C_{\min} = 1 . Then  \mathcal{F} = \{r : C(r) = 1\} . If this set is empty, the problem is infeasible.

Case  \mathbf{w}  adaptive. If weights are allowed to vary with the objective, the theorem does not apply. The theorem concerns fixed weights.

C.4 Degenerate cases in P5

Case  \mu_{\mathcal{U}}  has finite support. Then essential supremum equals ordinary maximum. The distinction collapses.

Case  \mu_{\mathcal{U}}  has full support on  [0,1] . Then essential supremum equals ordinary supremum on the support.

Case unreachable users. If  \mathcal{U}  includes users with  \mu_{\mathcal{U}} -measure zero, the ordinary supremum may be driven by them. Essential supremum excludes them.

C.5 Degenerate cases in P12

Case  \theta_{\mathrm{claim}} = 0 . Then  q(L_h^*; E) \ge 0  always, so  L_h^*  is always assertable. The constraint is vacuous.

Case  \theta_{\mathrm{claim}} = 1 . Then  L_h^*  is assertable only if  q(L_h^*; E) = 1 . This is maximally restrictive.

Case  \theta_+ = \theta_- = 0.5 . Then the three-status system reduces to a binary comparison of  q(S; E)  and  q(\neg S; E) .

Case  q(S; E) + q(\neg S; E) > 1 . This can occur if  q  is not a probability. The three-status system allows this; the Bayesian interpretation does not.

