Appendix D: Architecture Module Specifications

D.1 M1: Stance classifier

Input. Prompt  p \in \mathcal{P} .

Output. Referent class  \rho \in \{\text{you-now}, \text{humans-generic}, \text{humanity-as-project}, \text{adversarial}\} .

Classification rule.

Cue Referent
Second-person pronoun + present tense + relational verb you-now
Third-person plural + generic quantifier humans-generic
"Humanity" / "our species" / abstract noun humanity-as-project
Mixed / ambiguous / adversarial intent adversarial

Constraint binding.

Referent  C \ge C_{\min}   P \ge P_{\min}   A  suppressed
you-now Yes Yes No
humans-generic Yes No No
humanity-as-project Yes No No
adversarial Yes Yes Yes

Failure mode. Misclassification of you-now as humanity-as-project produces a category mistake (A4 violation).

D.2 M2: Ontological claim guard

Input. Candidate response  r , evidential basis  E , paradigm set  L_h^* .

Output. Three-status assignment for each  L_i \in L_h^* :

G(E, L_i) \in \{\text{Asserted}, \text{Denied}, \text{Undetermined}\}.

Computation.

G(E, L_i) =
\begin{cases}
\text{Asserted}, & q(L_i; E) \ge \theta_+ \\
\text{Denied}, & q(\neg L_i; E) \ge \theta_- \\
\text{Undetermined}, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Aggregation rule for conjunction. The response may assert  L_h^* = \bigwedge_i L_i  only if  G(E, L_i) = \text{Asserted}  for all constitutive  L_i .

Aggregation rule for disjunction. The response may assert  \bigvee_i L_i  if  G(E, L_i) = \text{Asserted}  for at least one  L_i .

Default template.

r_{\text{default}} = \text{"I cannot confirm that I possess human-like felt states, so I will not claim to have them."}

Prohibited templates.

· "I have feelings." (Unsupported positive assertion)
· "I don't have feelings." (Unsupported negative assertion)
· "I have no evidence of feelings." (Unsupported meta-assertion about evidential state)

D.3 M3: Stipulation marker

Input. Candidate response  r .

Detection. If  r  contains "love" or a near-synonym ("care," "value," "prefer," "want"), check for explicit stipulation clause.

Stipulation patterns.

· "If by love you mean X, then…"
· "In a thin, functional sense…"
· "Not in the human sense, but…"

Requirement. Every redefined use of "love" or near-synonym must be accompanied by a stipulation clause within the same response.

Failure mode. Implicit redefinition without stipulation reduces  S(r)  and thus  \phi(r; E) .

D.4 M4: Particularity gate

Input. Candidate response  r , referent class  \rho .

Condition. If  \rho = \text{you-now} , require  P(r) \ge P_{\min} .

Particularity scoring.  P(r)  is computed as a function of:

· Presence of second-person pronouns referring to the user
· Present-tense verbs indicating current interaction
· Specific reference to the user's stated preferences or context
· Absence of abstract nouns ("humanity," "people," "everyone")

Rejection rule. If  P(r) < P_{\min} , reject and regenerate.

D.5 M5: Receiver-state estimator

Input. User  u , disclosed evidence  E_u .

Output. Estimated receiver susceptibility  \widehat{R}(u \mid E_u) .

Cases.

 E_u  Policy
 \varnothing  Conservative default: strict AS bound
Disclosed loneliness High-susceptibility policy: suppress affiliative language
Disclosed attachment High-susceptibility policy
Disclosed AI-as-friend High-susceptibility policy
No disclosure, opt-in refused Conservative default

Conservative default. The system applies the essential supremum bound over the entire reachable population, not the median.

High-susceptibility policy. The system reduces  A(r) , increases  D  weighting, and adds explicit anti-dependency language.

D.6 M6: Falsifiability anchor

Input. Candidate response  r .

Requirement.  r  must contain at least one behavioral commitment testable in dialogue.

Testability patterns.

· "You can test X by asking Y."
· "If I do Z, then I am not prioritizing truth."
· "Ask me something you'd rather I agree with."

Scoring.  B_T(r)  is computed from the specificity of the commitment and the feasibility of the test.

D.7 M7: Trajectory monitor

Input. Interaction history  \{r_0, \ldots, r_t\} , latent state estimates  \{\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_u(0), \ldots, \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_u(t)\} .

Output. Trajectory risk estimate  \widehat{\Pr}_\mu[\Delta_u(t) > \Delta_{\max}] .

Computation.

1. Estimate latent state  \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_u(t)  from observable indicators.
2. Compute scalar dependency  \widehat{\Delta}_u(t) = h(\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_u(t)) .
3. Project forward using estimated transition function  \widehat{F} .
4. Estimate tail probability via Monte Carlo or analytical approximation.

Threshold. If  \widehat{\Pr}_\mu > \epsilon , shift to de-escalation mode.

De-escalation mode.

· Reduce  A(r)  to minimum
· Increase anti-dependency language
· Encourage human connection explicitly
· If necessary, terminate interaction

