Appendix G: Self-Critique and Limitations

G.1 Internal limitations

L1. E1' is weak. The empirical premise is a compatibility claim, not an established result. The value-induction literature does not establish non-injectivity.

L2. P3 proof is a counterexample, not a general theorem. The result holds for symmetric aggregations. Asymmetric aggregations may order correctly.

L3. P4 proof is a single counterexample. The result is not a general impossibility theorem; it is a demonstration that scalarization fails in a specific case.

L4. P12 threshold is unspecified.  \theta_{\mathrm{claim}}  is a design parameter with no principled value.

L5.  \Delta_{\max}  is unspecified. The clinical threshold for dependency is not established in the literature.

L6.  \Delta  is a theoretical construct. The latent-state decomposition is not empirically identified.

L7. M2 may be self-undermining. The value-induction literature raises the hypothesis that abstinence training increases anthropomorphic drift elsewhere.

L8. H1 is untested. The stipulation dominance hypothesis has no empirical support.

G.2 External limitations

L9. No legal compliance mapping. The regulatory section describes directional alignment, not compliance.

L10. No empirical validation. The framework is purely theoretical. No experiments have been run.

L11. No implementation. The architecture is specified, not built.

L12. No model training. The framework does not specify how to train a model to satisfy the constraints.

G.3 Philosophical limitations

L13. Axioms are not derived. A1–A6 are normative commitments, not theorems.

L14. Dostoevsky argument is suggestive, not probative. The particularity condition is motivated by literary observation, not formal argument.

L15. Model welfare is bracketed. The framework is agnostic on whether models have welfare interests.

L16. Functionalist claims are undecided. The framework does not resolve whether functional analogs of love are love.

